<section id="fit"><h2>What fits, and what’s tight</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3 class="ok">Fits well</h3><ul>
<li><b>Steinway B</b> (6′11″ × 4′10″): keys toward the kitchen, tail toward the front windows, lid opening west toward the fireplace lounge. 3′5″ to the lounge, 4′1″ to the stair rail, 5′7″ from the tail to the windows.</li>
<li><b>Cigar chairs + tufted bench</b> face each other across the hearth, about 2½ ft off the fireplace.</li>
<li><b>Speakeasy</b> (9′2″ × 13′6″) behind the hinged bookcase, kept as it is (existing walls, ceiling and floor, plus a rug); Jenny’s own pieces, to scale: a 42″ × 42″ high-top table, 36″ tall, in dark espresso wood with a diagonal four-way veneer top, a thick apron and square legs, centered in the room (about 34″ to each side wall and about 5 ft to each end), with her 4 matching counter-height chairs (dark brown wood, taupe upholstered seats, tall slatted backs; about 16″ behind the side chairs to the wall, so it’s snug). Her bar cart (honey-oak frame, beige cultured-marble inset top, 28.5″ × 17.5″, about 34.5″ tall, black wire wine rack on top) sits against the far end wall, about 3′6″ from the table. The bookcase entry is at the other end, and its swing (about 3 ft) stays clear by about 7″.</li>
<li><b>Secondary office in basement storage:</b> the room stays unfinished; the glass desk + Aeron sit in the center, partitioned by freestanding IKEA shelves and a curtain, with every mechanical system left open to reach. <a href="#basement">Plan and photos</a>.</li>
<li><b>Bedroom 2 (kids’ room, 13′9″ × 13′6″):</b> full bed on the north wall, nightstand, kids desk, 6-drawer dresser.</li>
<li><b>Bedroom 3 (teen room, later guest, 17′11″ × 10′5″):</b> sleigh bed, nightstand, dresser, small desk.</li>
<li><b>Primary:</b> king bed on the back wall between nightstands, two tall chests. <b>Dining:</b> table with 6 chairs. <b>Kitchen:</b> 4 counter stools and a buffet by the pantry.</li>
<li><b>TV, console, soundbar, sub</b> on the existing family-room TV wall (outlet and cable are already there).</li>
<li><b>One BendPak HD-9XW-class lift</b> (high-rise, 9,000 lb) in the right bay: Model Y under, 911 Carrera 4S on top at about a 72 in lock. From the photos, the right door is about 10 ft × 8 ft with a torsion spring over the door, standard-lift tracks and a wall-mount (jackshaft) opener, so there’s no ceiling opener rail. The horizontal tracks should end about 9′9″–10′ in. The lift’s front posts sit about 10 ft in, just behind the track ends, so the 911 on top sits behind the open door. The Model Y parks nose-in just inside the door, with its front end under the lift. A high-lift track conversion (easy with the wall-mount opener) would let the lift move about 3.5 ft closer to the door. The XT5 parks in the left bay without a lift, which frees the left wall for the backup-power equipment.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="tight">Tight / check</h3><ul>
<li><b>Sectional: 14′ × 10′6″ overall (measured, Nov 25).</b> Same back-wall corner as before: the 14′ leg runs along the back wall from the bookcase-side corner, and the 10′6″ leg returns toward the TV at its far end (about 7 ft in from the bath wall). Turned this way, nothing sits in front of the hinged bookcase: the sectional’s nearest end is about 5 ft from it, so the secret door swings freely. The short leg ends about 5′5″ short of the TV console, which leaves the walk from the stair to the bookcase open, and the massage chair keeps its spot by the bath wall. The other way round, with the 10′6″ leg down the bookcase wall, the L would cover the bookcase door. Seat depth (about 40″ in the model) and the individual piece sizes are not measured yet.</li>
<li><b>TV distance:</b> about 15 ft from the back-wall seats to the 75″ screen, which is centered on the 14′ leg; the seats on the short leg are about 9–12 ft away, at an angle. 15 ft is on the far side for a 75″. If it feels far, float the L about 2–3 ft off the back wall with a sofa table behind (about 12–13 ft), or go 85″.</li>
<li><b>Massage chair</b> needs ~6½ ft when reclined; it sits on the angle by the bath wall.</li>
<li><b>Piano and sun:</b> keep the tail 5+ ft from the front windows (as shown), use UV film or shades, and add a humidity system (Dampp-Chaser).</li>
<li><b>Do not raise the lift to the top lock</b> until the ceiling is taped. Confirm the exact drive-under with BendPak and the post spacing against the stair landing (about 0–0.5 ft of margin).</li>
<li><b>Door tracks:</b> the side doors look about 10 × 8 and the center about 12 × 12 in the front photo. The right bay’s standard-lift tracks likely end about 9′9″–10′ in at about 8′3″–8′6″ high, so the lift’s front posts sit at about 10 ft (the posts are about 8′4″ tall and can’t go under the tracks). Tape the track end before you set the lift. Confidence: door sizes medium-high; track length medium-low.</li>
<li><b>High rings and rope:</b> the trusses are not designed for these loads as built. Get a written letter from the truss maker or an engineer before hanging anything. Never drill, notch or hang from a truss chord or web. The rope tops out at about 12′4″–12′6″.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="no">Still to confirm</h3><ul><li>Existing pieces left with the house are placed at approximate sizes until they are measured.</li><li>Primary bed wall, Bedroom 2 / 3 orientation and the speakeasy layout are checked against the walkthrough videos in the second pass.</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section id="move"><h2>Move-in routes for the piano and sectional</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Piano</h3><p>Easiest through the <b>6 ft rear French doors</b> in the dining room off the covered deck, then about 15 ft to its spot. Second choice is the 36″ front door with the sidelight; a piano mover takes it in on its side on a board either way. No stairs inside.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route A: main stair</h3><p>Straight run, 15 risers, ~4′1″ framed width (~3′5″–3′8″ clear), landing in an 8′3″ hall with room to turn, then open into the family room. <b>Best route</b>, but a <b>stair lift</b> shows on this stair in the listing photos; if it stays, its rail costs about a foot of width. Also check the headroom where the stair passes under the main floor. The 14′ × 10′6″ L comes in sections; the piece sizes aren’t measured yet, but sections up to about 40″ deep should pass flat on this stair once the stair lift is gone, and long sections need the landing turn checked.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route B: garage stair</h3><p>From the right side of the garage: a straight 14-riser stair in a 3′11″ hall, then a <b>90° turn through a ~36″ door</b> at the bottom into the storage room, then a 36″ door into the family room. No finished floors to protect, but the bottom turn is the pinch point: pieces deeper than ~34″ or longer than the landing ceiling may not pivot. For our 14′ × 10′6″ L, pieces longer than about 6–7 ft are unlikely to make this turn, so plan on route A unless the piece sizes say otherwise. Measure before moving day.</p></div></div></section>

