<section id="garage"><h2>Garage: CrossFit gym, one lift, mini office at the south window (v6)</h2>
<p>Updated Nov 24, 2026 (plan v6). The 40×50 heated garage has 10 ft walls rising to a flat center of about <b>13 ft</b> over the 12×12 door, with about 11–12 ft at the side-bay centerlines, and a storage loft over the <b>center of the back wall only</b> (about 12–27 ft from the south wall, per the listing photos), with about 8 ft clear under it; the loft is fixed and <b>hangs from the ceiling</b> on white boxed hangers, with <b>no floor posts</b>, so the floor under it is fully open. Our stand-up paddleboard (about 11 ft) and two kayaks (about 10½ ft each) from the FL home ride on the loft deck, which is about 15 ft long. The back-left gray workbench and cabinet run stays. All the gray steel shelving is movable and goes to basement storage, and the sellers’ tire rack leaves, which frees the back-right spot. The gym is about <b>670 sf</b>. The 8×8 lifting platform, our own black Xebex-style rack, squared up as a 4-post rig of about 5 × 5 ft with pull-up bars at the head of the platform, and the high rings and climbing rope sit in the 13 ft zone. The cold plunge and a GHD / reverse hyper sit beside it. Cardio runs along the left bay: our <b>Xebex Runner Smart Connect</b> curved treadmill (71″ × 35″ × 60″, no power needed) and our <b>EnergyFit Ski-Row Air</b> (103″ × 33″ as a rower; it flips up to a 54″-long, 84″-tall ski-erg). Optional later: a <b>Woodway RidgeRunner</b> incline trainer (35″ × 93½″, 72″ tall, 90–96″ at full incline, 505 lb) fits the same slot but needs its own 20A circuit and has less than the recommended 79″ clear behind it. The <b>wall-ball target</b> (10 ft line) mounts on the loft’s front face at its front-left hanger, about 1.3 ft above the ~8.3 ft deck edge, with a 3½ ft throwing zone in front on the mats. Our <b>Rogue Floor Glute</b> (62″ × 24″) for Nordic curls sits under the loft between the air bike and the GHD, with a 7 ft clear fall-forward lane. Under the fixed loft only low racks: dumbbells and kettlebells, plates, the bar tree and plyo boxes; the bench and hip-thrust station take the old tire-rack spot. The walk of about 45 in to the house door stays clear.</p>
<p><b>Stretch / bodyweight / TV area (shared):</b> open floor in the far back-left corner, beside the existing gray workbench and cabinets, with no loft over it (about 10–12 ft). A wall rack holds rollers, bands, mats and light dumbbells, the TV hangs on the south wall, and the futon sits about 8 ft back from the TV, facing it, so the bodyweight floor between them stays clear. <b>Open office (no built walls):</b> about 10 × 9′8″ in front of the rear south window, defined IKEA small-space style by furniture: two PAX wardrobes (39″ × 23″ × 79″) and an IVAR cabinet (31″ × 20″ × 33″) on the cardio side, an open-back KALLAX 4×4 (58″ × 15″ × 58″) on the gym side with an open entry, a KALLAX 2×4 on its side toward the stretch area, and a VIDGA ceiling-track curtain that slides open. The new standing desk faces the window. <b>Cars:</b> one 4-post lift in the right bay, set about 10 ft in so it clears the open door, Model Y under and 911 on top. The XT5 parks in the left bay without a lift, and the Airstream stays in the 12×12. A Tesla Wall Connector is planned on the front wall pier of the right bay. <b>Backup power:</b> three FranklinWH batteries and the aGate on the left wall, with 36 in kept clear; a starter solar array fills the garage south roof, and an outdoor 240V inlet takes a portable dual-fuel generator. Final layout with the installer. <b>Heat and light:</b> the existing gas unit heater and the existing garage lighting stay; nothing added. <b>Cold plunge:</b> The Plunge tub (66⅝″ × 31½″ × 26″) with its chiller; the maker says its chiller needs an outlet on a <b>dedicated 120V / 15A circuit</b>. <b>Outlets:</b> the builder plans show no garage receptacles, so the plan marks the outlets seen or implied in the listing photos (back wall by the shelving, house wall by the door switches, the workbench backsplash, the freezer by the stair) and the door-opener outlets at ceiling height. No 30A RV or 240V outlet shows. Add a dedicated 15–20A GFCI outlet by the plunge, a 30A RV outlet (the Airstream’s spec) on the pier between the left and center doors, next to its cord inlet, and a 20A circuit if a RidgeRunner comes; the Wall Connector and lift get their own circuits.</p>
<p><b>High rings and climbing rope (concept, Nov 23):</b> the look is a visible <b>wood or steel mounting bar</b> across the 13 ft ceiling, with the gymnastic rings (straps about 20 in apart, over the 8×8 platform) and a 1.5 in manila climbing rope (with a swivel, over a crash mat) hanging from it. The garage roof is trussed at about 24 in on center, and the attic hatch is at the center of the garage, so the support is built from above: a long engineered beam (a strongback) laid across several trusses in the attic and tied in with structural screws, blocking and bracing, then through-bolted to the visible bar below. Spreading the load this way means no single truss carries a swinging athlete. It is sized for two people at once (one on the rings, one on the rope) with a large dynamic and safety margin. <b>Nothing gets installed without a written letter from the truss manufacturer or an engineer.</b> If they say no, the fallback is a taller freestanding frame bolted to the slab. The rig and the bar stay clear of the fixed loft edge.</p>
