<section id="prefab-sauna"><h2>Prefab cedar sauna: deck or garage gym (three sizes)</h2>
<p>Updated Nov 27, 2026 (concept). A modular, electric cedar sauna that comes in three sizes, as an alternative to the skid sauna below. We looked at two spots: the <b>west end of the covered deck, opposite the hot tub</b>, and the <b>garage gym, beside the cold plunge</b>. <b>Tap any photo</b> to open it full-size.</p>
<table class="costs"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Footprint (W × D)</th><th>Height</th><th>Seats</th><th>Power</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>XL</td><td>7′8″ × 5′11″</td><td>7′11″</td><td>up to 7</td><td>own 240V / 50A circuit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Standard</td><td>5′8″ × 5′11″</td><td>7′11″</td><td>up to 5</td><td>own 240V / 30A circuit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mini</td><td>4′4″ × 4′9″</td><td>7′10″</td><td>2</td><td>own 240V / 20A circuit</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>None of them can use the deck’s existing 120V outlets or share the hot tub’s circuit; each needs its own new 240V circuit and outlet, planned with the installer.</p>
<h3>On the deck, opposite the hot tub</h3>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/ps-xl-yard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/ps-xl-yard.jpg" alt="XL sauna at the west end of the covered deck, seen from the yard"></a><figcaption>XL from the yard: the hot tub at the left end of the deck, the sauna at the right end beside the garage man door, glass front toward the yard (concept)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/ps-std-yard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/ps-std-yard.jpg" alt="Standard sauna in the same deck spot, same camera"></a><figcaption>Standard in the same spot and from the same camera, for comparison: one front glass panel narrower (concept)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/ps-xl-front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/ps-xl-front.jpg" alt="XL sauna close up from the lawn"></a><figcaption>XL close up from the lawn at the deck corner: the glass front and door, the garage man door just beyond, the sectional to the left (concept)</figcaption></figure></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Placement:</b> against the garage end of the deck, glass front facing the yard and woods, set 2 ft in from the deck edge so the door can swing (24″ clear). The glass could face the hot tub instead.</li>
<li><b>Walkway behind:</b> about 4 ft to the house wall (about 3 ft if the deck is 11 ft deep rather than 12; tape it). The Mini leaves about 5 ft.</li>
<li><b>Sectional:</b> the XL sits about 2′4″ from the sectional’s return, so the sectional shifts 1–2 ft toward the hot tub; the Standard leaves about 4′4″, the Mini about 5 ft.</li>
<li><b>View:</b> the XL covers the kitchen window at that end, the Standard most of it, the Mini about 60%.</li>
<li><b>Height is the go/no-go:</b> 7′10″–7′11″ tall under a porch ceiling of about 8 ft (estimated). The beam and shade housing are lower. Tape the ceiling at that end before buying, and leave room to set the roof panels.</li>
<li><b>Deck load:</b> the maker asks for a surface that holds at least 1,400 lb. A full XL works out to roughly 57 lb/sq ft, above a typical 50 lb/sq ft deck design, so have the framing checked and add blocking or a post under it (Mini about 48 lb/sq ft).</li>
</ul>
<h3>In the garage gym, beside the cold plunge</h3>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/ps-garage-plan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/ps-garage-plan.jpg" alt="Garage rear-right corner plan with a Mini sauna beside the cold plunge"></a><figcaption>Rear-right corner of the garage, to scale: the Mini 2 ft behind the cold plunge, its glass door facing the stair, the GHD moved to the back wall; the dashed box is the Standard (concept plan, tape on site)</figcaption></figure></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Mini: fits cleanly.</b> In the GHD spot behind the plunge, just outside the loft, with about 41″ left for the walk to the back door. The GHD moves to the back wall and the bench moves over; parking, the lift, the platform and the house-door walk don’t change.</li>
<li><b>Standard: fits, but tight.</b> Same spot, but the walk to the back door narrows to about 28″, a little tighter than today’s 30″ pinch at the plunge.</li>
<li><b>XL: no clean spot near the plunge.</b> It would block the back-door walk or the house-door walk, and it can’t go under the 8 ft loft. Its garage option is the stretch / TV corner, about 20 ft from the plunge.</li>
<li><b>Why the garage is easier:</b> the ceiling there is about 11½–12½ ft (no height worry), the floor is the concrete slab (no deck reinforcement), the garage is heated, and the plunge is two steps away for hot-cold rounds. The wiring is a simpler job too.</li>
<li><b>Garage cautions:</b> no engines running while it’s in use, keep the maker’s heater and vent clearances, set it on the bare slab or a cut-out in the mats, and use a GFCI-protected outlet.</li>
</ul></section>
<section id="sauna"><h2>Portable 8×13 skid sauna off the deck</h2>
<p>Updated Nov 24, 2026 (rev. B, after a detailed plan review). A wood-fired sauna built on skids, styled like the rear storage shed so it matches the house: light gray lap siding, wide white trim, a white soffit, a single-slope shingled roof, and black windows and doors. It stands on its own <b>4 ft off the hot-tub end of the covered deck</b>, fully outside the deck’s roofline, so the two roofs never meet. A short 4 ft composite <b>boardwalk</b> runs from the deck edge (a small step down) to a small <b>changing room</b>, which keeps the heat in, then through a glass door into the <b>hot room</b>. It is a <b>portable structure</b>: under 200 sf, on skids with tow and lift points, on a gravel pad with no foundation and no hard utilities.</p>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-side.jpg" alt="Skid sauna standing on its own beside the covered deck, linked by a short boardwalk"></a><figcaption>From the side: the sauna stands on its own 4 ft off the hot-tub end of the covered deck, outside its roofline with open sky between the two roofs; a short composite boardwalk runs from the deck edge to the changing-room door (concept)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg" alt="Backyard site plan with the sauna 4 ft off the deck"></a><figcaption>Site plan, to scale: the sauna 4 ft off the hot-tub corner of the deck with the boardwalk between, the sketched septic reserve and the drain field. Open the <a href="explorer.html?view=deck" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3D model</a> to walk around it.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-hotroom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-hotroom.jpg" alt="Hot room with two full-length benches and a guarded wood stove"></a><figcaption>Hot room: two 7 ft benches, and a stove fed from outside behind a guard rail, with a tile heat shield on the stove wall, a tile hearth and a low fresh-air vent (concept)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/sauna-plan-8x13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/sauna-plan-8x13.jpg" alt="8 by 13 sauna floor plan"></a><figcaption>Floor plan: changing room at the deck end, hot room beyond</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Size and layout</h3><ul>
<li><b>Footprint:</b> 8′-0″ × 13′-0″ framed (104 sf), roof about 9′-6″ × 14′-0″ with overhangs; about 10 ft tall at the high side.</li>
<li><b>Changing room:</b> about 7′-1″ × 4′-9″, with a bench and hooks; a 32 in half-glass door faces the deck and boardwalk (black out-swing, or a painted in-swing door: still open).</li>
<li><b>Hot room:</b> about 7′-0″ × 7′-1″, for 2–3 people, behind a 24 × 74 in glass sauna door.</li>
<li><b>Benches:</b> two full-length benches, wall to wall, 84 in long: an <b>upper bench 24 in deep at 38 in</b> and a <b>lower bench 22 in deep at 18 in</b>. Two people can lie flat at once, one per bench; three sit comfortably.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Stove and fire protection</h3><ul>
<li>A thru-wall wood sauna stove, fired from the yard end so no ash or wood comes through the changing room: a <b>Harvia M3 SL</b> (the thru-wall model, not the plain M3) or a <b>Nippa WB-18 with its firing extension</b>. <b>Pick the stove before framing</b>: the stove wall is designed from its manual.</li>
<li>The yard-end wall around the firing tunnel is <b>non-combustible</b> (steel studs with cement board, or masonry). The manual calls for an opening at least about 210 mm wide × 390 mm high, in a wall no thicker than 150 mm, with the non-combustible wall continuing beside the opening and up to the roof. Fire-retardant lumber is still combustible, so it is not the fire protection.</li>
<li>Ventilated tile-on-cement-board heat shield behind and beside the stove, a tile hearth pad, a wood guard rail, and only the stove maker’s listed clearances.</li>
<li>6 in insulated Class-A chimney with a spark-arrestor cap. With the stove at the low wall, clearing everything within 10 ft by 2 ft puts the cap <b>about 44 in above the roof</b> where it passes through (braced if it ever stands more than 5 ft above the roof).</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Air, roof and floor</h3><ul>
<li><b>Ventilation:</b> an adjustable fresh-air intake low on the wall near the stove and an adjustable exhaust on the opposite wall (under the bench or high, per the stove maker).</li>
<li><b>Roof:</b> 2x8 rafters with R-23 mineral wool leave about a 1¾ in air channel from the vented low soffit to a continuous vent at the high eave. The alternative is 2x10 rafters with R-30. Designed for the area’s 60 psf ground snow load; the builder verifies.</li>
<li><b>Floor:</b> 2 in foam insulation between the joists and a waterproof floor sloped to a drain or scupper onto the gravel, with duckboards in the hot room.</li>
<li><b>Height:</b> the hot-room ceiling runs about 6′-4″ to 8′-0″ after lining, so the upper bench has about 4′-10″ of head room. The 8 ft high side is tall for a 7×7 room; lowering it or raising the benches is still open.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Portable by design</h3><ul>
<li>Three pressure-treated skids with tow eyes at both ends and four corner lift points; it can be towed or craned out. Tow plates, bolts and lift angles are sized by a fabricator or engineer for a 4,000–5,000 lb building, all hot-dip galvanized.</li>
<li>Sits on a level gravel pad with optional removable ground anchors. No foundation, no water, no hard-wired power.</li>
<li>Placed 4 ft off the deck at the hot-tub corner, running 13 ft into the yard, with the flue at the yard end, well away from the house. The septic reserve is only sketched and may reach the pad, so it is marked before anything is staked.</li>
<li><b>Approvals:</b> a one-story detached building under 200 sq ft is exempt from a Michigan building permit, but a township zoning or land-use permit and setbacks usually still apply. Confirm HOA and wood-stove rules, and tell the home insurer about the wood stove.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Still to decide</h3><ol>
<li>Which stove (it sets the stove-wall detail).</li>
<li>High-wall height and bench heights.</li>
<li>Roof: 2x8 with R-23, or 2x10 with R-30.</li>
<li>Lining: thin plank, or proper sauna-grade tongue-and-groove.</li>
<li>Entry door: paint a primed in-swing door, or order a black out-swing door.</li>
<li>Where the septic reserve actually is, then township zoning.</li>
</ol></div>
</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Plans (PDF)</h3><ul>
<li><a href="docs/687-portable-skid-sauna-8x13-concept.pdf"><b>687 portable skid sauna, 8×13 plan set</b></a>: floor plan, elevations, section, framing, bench detail, stove wall and chimney fire protection, ventilation, skids and tow points, site placement, and a materials list (rev. B, Nov 24). Concept, verify with the builder.</li>
<li><a href="docs/sauna-reference-set-9x16.pdf">Original 9×16 skid sauna framing set</a> that the 687 plans adapt (reference only).</li>
</ul></div>
</section>

<section id="outdoors"><h2>Trails from the back door</h2><p>The west lot line borders state forest. An old two-track from the backyard reaches the Vasa Singletrack in about half a mile, and the Sand Lakes Quiet Area, Muncie Lakes and the main Vasa trailhead are each a short drive.</p>
<div class="full"><figure><a href="img/687-trails-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/687-trails-map.jpg" alt="Trail access near 687 Starflower Ln"></a><figcaption>Trail access near 687 Starflower Ln</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section id="measure"><h2>Measurements to take on the next visit</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Our furniture (Florida)</h3><ol><li>Every sectional piece: length × depth × height, and which pieces unbolt (overall is 14′ × 10′6″, measured Nov 25).</li><li>Cigar chairs, tufted bench, media console, kids desk.</li><li>Massage chair, upright and fully reclined.</li><li>Bed frame and headboard height; the carved mirror panel.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card"><h3>At 687</h3><ol>
<li>Main stair: clear width rail-to-wall, headroom at the bottom, and whether the stair lift stays.</li>
<li>Garage stair: clear width, bottom landing length, ceiling height there, door clear width.</li>
<li>Angled family-room door and the bookcase-door opening (clear width).</li>
<li>Front door and rear French doors, clear width.</li>
<li>Floor vents and outlets near the piano spot; coax and power on the TV wall.</li>
<li>Center garage door clear opening (for the Airstream).</li>
<li><b>Tape the garage ceiling:</b> wall height in each bay, height at each bay centerline, and the flat center / peak. The drawings say 10 ft walls and about 13′8″ in the center; the exterior ridge is taller.</li>
<li>Confirm the HD-9XW (110.25 in overall width, 100.25 in posts) in the right bay, including a ~2 ft shift toward the ridge. Tape the ceiling along those shifted centerlines. Confirm the lift’s front posts and the 911 on top against the 8-ft door’s overhead tracks. Measure the Model Y with and without a rack against the ~72 in lock.</li>
<li>Wall Connector cable length once the lift is placed; confirm it reaches the Model Y driver-rear port from the front wall pier of the right bay.</li>
<li>Open the garage ceiling hatch (carefully) and note whether it opens into the shallow garage scissor cavity or reaches the house attic; look for a second access in the house closets; check whether the lid is a 20-minute / self-closing or 5/8″ Type X hatch.</li>
<li>Stake the 8×13 sauna 4 ft off the hot-tub corner of the deck, 13 ft long running into the yard: tape the deck height above grade and the grade for a level gravel skid pad that puts the changing-room floor about 2 in below the deck, and lay out the 4 ft boardwalk between them.</li><li>Have a pumper mark the septic tank, drain field and reserve, and keep the sauna and its pad off them.</li><li>Check for trees or anything within 10 ft of the flue at the yard end (the cap sits about 44 in above the sauna roof), and confirm the deck railing opening by the hot tub.</li><li>Ask the township about zoning or land-use permits and setbacks for a 104 sq ft accessory building with a wood stove.</li>
</ol></div>
<div class="card"><h3>How confident is this?</h3><ul><li><b>Main floor: high.</b> Permit drawings and the listing plan agree.</li><li><b>Lower level: medium-high.</b> Room sizes are from the listing plan, interior walls ±1 ft.</li><li><b>Garage: medium.</b> Shell from the drawings, contents from the gym plan. Ceiling height is medium-high at the 10 ft walls, medium at the 13′8″ center until it’s taped.</li><li><b>Septic areas: low.</b> Approximate, from the permit sketch.</li></ul></div></div></section>
